 "amendments": {
  "items": {
   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the correction law, in relation to screening incarcerated\nindividuals for eligibility in the supplemental nutrition assistance program\nprior to release",
    "basePrintNo": "S8173",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S8173-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [
      {
       "alternate": false,
       "chamber": "SENATE",
       "districtCode": 52,
       "fullName": "Lea Webb",
       "imgName": "1332_Lea_Webb.jpg",
       "incumbent": true,
       "memberId": 1515,
       "sessionMemberId": 1878,
       "sessionYear": 2023,
       "shortName": "WEBB"
      }
     ],
     "size": 1
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add §619-a, Cor L",
    "lawSection": "Correction Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S8173\n\nSPONSOR: BAILEY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the correction law, in relation to screening incarcerat-\ned individuals for eligibility in the supplemental nutrition assistance\nprogram prior to release\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nRequires screening incarcerated individuals for eligibility in the\nsupplemental nutrition assistance program prior to release.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 amends correction law by adding a new section 619-a, which\nstates it should be the duty of an official of any institution under the\njurisdiction of the commissioner to cooperate with an authorized agency\nof the department of social services to screen incarcerated individuals\nfor eligibility for the supplemental nutrition access program pursuant\nto section 95 of the social services law 30 days prior to release and to\nassist the individuals in applying for the program. It states that the\nofficial, in cooperation with the authorized agency, should seek any\nnecessary waivers from the United States Department of Agriculture to\nsuspend the 3 month time limit for individuals in areas without work and\nto ensure that incarcerated individuals have 1 year of eligibility upon\nrelease.\n\nSection 2 sets the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAccording to the Center on Budget and Policy Priorities, more than\n600,000 people are released from state and federal prisons each year.\nFormerly incarcerated individuals face many obstacles in re-entering\ntheir communities, including food insecurity, unstable housing, and\ndifficulties in finding and retaining employment. Due to these chal-\nlenges, recidivism can become common. According to a study that looked\ninto the release of incarcerated individuals from state prisons across\n30 states, the U.S. Bureau of Justice Statistics found that approximate-\nly 68% were rearrested within three years of their release and approxi-\nmately 77% within five years of their release.\n\nAccess to basic social services such as food assistance can help incar-\ncerated individuals reenter their communities and avoid cycling in and\nout of the criminal justice system. Lack of access to food is the imme-\ndiate issue that most individuals leaving the system experience. They\nare twice as likely to suffer food insecurity as the general population,\nwith 1 in 5 formerly incarcerated individuals finding it difficult to\nobtain regular meals according to the Prison Policy Initiative. The\nSupplemental Nutrition Assistance Program (SNAP) has been shown to help\nreduce food insecurity. A study done by the U.S. Department of Agricul-\nture found that receiving SNAP benefits decreases the probability of\nbeing food insecure by about 30% and the probability of being very food\ninsecure by about 20%.\n\nTo avoid recidivism and to achieve successful reentry into their commu-\nnities, it requires being able to meet basic needs such as access to\nfood which has led to states like Illinois and California to expand\nand/or put into place pre-release SNAP benefits programs this year,\nrespectively. This legislation would seek to ensure that incarcerated\nindividuals have the opportunity to apply for SNAP benefits 30 days\nbefore they are released which will allow them to begin to acquire\nstability in their lives and to support themselves and their families\nonce they are back in their communities.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
